n    49→_RESERVED_CROSSORIGIN_VALUES = frozenset({'anonymous', 'use-credentials'})\n    50→\n    51→_RESERVED_SAMESITE_VALUES = frozenset({'lax', 'strict', 'none'})\n    52→\n    53→\n    54→class Response:\n    55→    \"\"\"Represents an HTTP response to a client request.\n    56→\n    57→    Note:\n    58→        ``Response`` is not meant to be instantiated directly by responders.\n    59→\n    60→    Keyword Arguments:\n    61→        options (dict): Set of global options passed from the App handler.\n    62→\n    63→    Attributes:\n    64→        status (Union[str,int]): HTTP status code or line (e.g., ``'200 OK'``).\n    65→            This may be set to a member of :class:`http.HTTPStatus`, an HTTP\n    66→            status line string or byte string (e.g., ``'200 OK'``), or an\n    67→            ``int``.\n    68→\n    69→            Note:\n    70→                The Falcon framework itself provides a number of constants for\n    71→                common status codes. They all start with the ``HTTP_`` prefix,\n    72→                as in: ``falcon.HTTP_204``. (See also: :ref:`status`.)\n    73→\n    74→        status_code (int): HTTP status code normalized from :attr:`status`.\n    75→            When a code is assigned to this property, :attr:`status` is updated,\n    76→            and vice-versa. The status code can be useful when needing to check\n    77→            in middleware for codes that fall into a certain class, e.g.::\n    78→\n    79→                if resp.status_code >= 400:\n    80→                    log.warning(f'returning error response: {resp.status_code}')\n    81→\n    82→        media (object): A serializable object supported by the media handlers\n    83→            configured via :class:`falcon.RequestOptions`.\n    84→\n    85→            Note:\n    86→                See also :ref:`media` for more information regarding media\n    87→                handling.\n    88→\n    89→        text (str): String representing response content.\n    90→\n    91→            Note:\n    92→                Falcon will encode the given text as UTF-8\n    93→                in the response. If the content is already a byte string,\n    94                use the :attr:`data` attribute instead (it's faster).\n    95→\n    96→        data (bytes): Byte string representing response content.\n    97→\n    98→            Use this attribute in lieu of `text` when your content is\n    99→            already a byte string (of type ``bytes``). See also the note below.\n   100→\n   101→            Warning:\n   102→                Always use the `text` attribute for text, or encode it\n   103→                first to ``bytes`` when using the `data` attribute, to\n   104→                ensure Unicode characters are properly encoded in the\n   105→                HTTP response.\n   106→\n   107→        stream: Either a file-like object with a `read()` method that takes\n   108→            an optional size argument and returns a block of bytes, or an\n   109→            iterable object, representing response content, and yielding\n   110→            blocks as byte strings. Falcon will use *wsgi.file_wrapper*, if\n   111→            provided by the WSGI server, in order to efficiently serve\n   112→            file-like objects.\n   113→\n   114→            Note:\n   115→                If the stream is set to an iterable object that requires\n   116→                resource cleanup, it can implement a close() method to do so.\n   117→                The close() method will be called upon completion of the request.\n   118→\n   119→        context (object): Empty object to hold any data (in its attributes)\n   120→            about the response which is specific to your app (e.g. session\n   121→            object). Falcon itself will not interact with this attribute after\n   122→            it has been initialized.\n   123→\n   124→            Note:\n   125→                **New in 2.0:** The default `context_type` (se