rmance file transmission when `resp.stream` is\n  1250→            set to a file-like object.\n  1251→        host(str): A string to use for the hostname part of the fully\n  1252→            qualified request URL (default: 'falconframework.org')\n  1253→        remote_addr (str): A string to use as the remote IP address for the\n  1254→            request (default: '127.0.0.1'). For WSGI, this corresponds to\n  1255→            the 'REMOTE_ADDR' environ variable. For ASGI, this corresponds\n  1256→            to the IP address used for the 'client' field in the connection\n  1257→            scope.\n  1258→        http_version (str): The HTTP version to simulate. Must be either\n  1259→            '2', '2.0', 1.1', '1.0', or '1' (default '1.1'). If set to '1.0',\n  1260→            the Host header will not be added to the scope.\n  1261→        wsgierrors (io): The stream to use as *wsgierrors* in the WSGI\n  1262→            environ (default ``sys.stderr``)\n  1263→        asgi_chunk_size (int): The maximum number of bytes that will be\n  1264→            sent to the ASGI app in a single ``'http.request'`` event (default\n  1265→            4096).\n  1266→        asgi_disconnect_ttl (int): The maximum number of seconds to wait\n  1267→            since the request was initiated, before emitting an\n  1268→            ``'http.disconnect'`` event when the app calls the\n  1269→            receive() function (default 300). Set to ``0`` to simulate an\n  1270→            immediate disconnection without first emitting ``'http.request'``.\n  1271→        extras (dict): Additional values to add to the WSGI\n  1272→            ``environ`` dictionary or the ASGI scope for the request\n  1273→            (default: ``None``)\n  1274→        cookies (dict): Cookies as a dict-like (Mapping) object, or an\n  1275→            iterable yielding a series of two-member (*name*, *value*)\n  1276→            iterables. Each pair of items provides the name and value\n  1277→            for the 'Set-Cookie' header.\n  1278→\n  1279→    Returns:\n  1280→        :py:class:`~.Result`: The result of the request\n  1281→    \"\"\"\n  1282→\n  1283→    return simulate_request(app, 'GET', path, **kwargs)\n  1284→\n  1285→\n  1286→def simulate_head(app, path, **kwargs) -> _ResultBase:\n  1287→    \"\"\"Simulate a HEAD request to a WSGI or ASGI application.\n  1288→\n  1289→    Equivalent to::\n  1290→\n  1291→         simulate_request(app, 'HEAD', path, **kwargs)\n  1292→\n  1293→    Note:\n  1294→        In the case of an ASGI request, this method will simulate the entire\n  1295→        app lifecycle in a single shot, including lifespan and client\n  1296→        disconnect events. In order to simulate multiple interleaved\n  1297→        requests, or to test a streaming endpoint (such as one that emits\n  1298→        server-sent events), :class:`~falcon.testing.ASGIConductor` can be\n  1299→        used to more precisely control the app lifecycle.\n  1300→\n  1301→    Args:\n  1302→        app (callable): The application to call\n  1303→        path (str): The URL path to request\n  1304→\n  1305→            Note:\n  1306→                The path may contain a query string. However, neither\n  1307→                `query_string` nor `params` may be specified in this case.\n  1308→\n  1309→    Keyword Args:\n  1310→        root_path (str): The initial portion of the request URL's \"path\" that\n  1311→            corresponds to the application object, so that the application\n  1312→            knows its virtual \"location\". This defaults to the empty string,\n  1313→            indicating that the application corresponds to the \"root\" of the\n  1314→            server.\n  1315→        protocol: The protocol to use for the URL scheme\n  1316→            (default: 'http')\n  1317→        port (int): The TCP port to simulate. Defaults to\n  1318→            the standard port used by the given scheme (i.e., 80 for 'http'\n  1319→            and 443 for 'https'). A string may also be passed, as long as\n  1320→            it can be parsed as an int.\n  1321→        params (dict): A dictionary of query string parameters,\n  1322→            where each key is a parameter name, and each value is\n  1323→            either a ``str`` or something that can be converted\n  1324→            into a ``str``, or a list of such values. If a ``list``,\n  1325→            the value will be converted to a comma-delimited string\n  1326→            of values (e.g., 'thing=1,2,3').\n  1327→        params_csv (bool): Set to ``True`` to encode list values\n  1328→            in query string params as comma-separated values\n  1329→            (e.g., 'thing=1,2,3'). Otherwise, parameters will be encoded by\n  1330→            specifying multiple instances of the parameter\n  1331→            (e.g., 'thing=1&thing=2&thing=3'). Defaults to ``False``.\n  1332→        query_string (str): A raw query string to include in the\n  1333→            request (default: ``None``). If specified, overrides\n  1334→            `params`.\n  1335→        headers (dict): Extra headers as a dict-like (Mapping) object, or an\n  1336→            iterable yielding a series of two-member (*name*, *value*)\n  1337→            iterables. Each pair of strings provides the name and value\n  1338→            for an HTTP header. If desired, multiple header values may be\n  1339→            combined into a single (*name*, *value*) pair by joining the values\n  1340→            with a comma when the header in question supports the list\n  1341→            format (see also RFC 7230 and RFC 7231). Header names are not\n  1342→            case-sensitive.\n  1343→\n  1344→            Note:\n  1345→                If a User-Agent header is not provided, it will default to::\n  1346→\n  1347→                    f'falcon-client/{falcon.__version__}'\n  1348→\n  1349→        host(str): A string to use for the hostname part of the fully\n  1350→            qualified reques