8→            stream: A readable file-like object.\n   329→            content_length (int): Length of the stream, used for the\n   330→                Content-Length header in the response.\n   331→        \"\"\"\n   332→\n   333→        self.stream = stream\n   334→\n   335→        # PERF(kgriffs): Set directly rather than incur the overhead of\n   336→        #   the self.content_length property.\n   337→        self._headers['content-length'] = str(content_length)\n   338→\n   339→    def set_cookie(\n   340→        self,\n   341→        name,\n   342→        value,\n   343→        expires=None,\n   344→        max_age=None,\n   345→        domain=None,\n   346→        path=None,\n   347→        secure=None,\n   348→        http_only=True,\n   349→        same_site=None,\n   350→        partitioned=None,\n   351→    ):\n   352→        \"\"\"Set a response cookie.\n   353→\n   354→        Note:\n   355→            This method can be called multiple times to add one or\n   356→            more cookies to the response.\n   357→\n   358→        See Also:\n   359→            To learn more about setting cookies, see\n   360→            :ref:`Setting Cookies <setting-cookies>`. The parameters\n   361→            listed below correspond to those defined in `RFC 6265`_.\n   362→\n   363→        Args:\n   364→            name (str): Cookie name\n   365→            value (str): Cookie value\n   366→\n   367→        Keyword Args:\n   368→            expires (datetime): Specifies when the cookie should expire.\n   369→                By default, cookies expire when the user agent exits.\n   370→\n   371→                (See also: RFC 6265, Section 4.1.2.1)\n   372→            max_age (int): Defines the lifetime of the cookie in\n   373→                seconds. By default, cookies expire when the user agent\n   374→                exits. If both `max_age` and `expires` are set, the\n   375→                latter is ignored by the user agent.\n   376→\n   377→                Note:\n   378→                    Coercion to ``int`` is attempted if provided with\n   379→                    ``float`` or ``str``.\n   380→\n   381→                (See also: RFC 6265, Section 4.1.2.2)\n   382→\n   383→            domain (str): Restricts the cookie to a specific domain and\n   384→                any subdomains of that domain. By default, the user\n   385→                agent will return the cookie only to the origin server.\n   386→                When overriding this default behavior, the specified\n   387→                domain must include the origin server. Otherwise, the\n   388→                user agent will reject the cookie.\n   389→\n   390→                Note:\n   391→                    Cookies do not provide isolation by port, so the domain\n   392→                    should not provide one. (See also: RFC 6265, Section 8.5)\n   393→\n   394→                (See also: RFC 6265, Section 4.1.2.3)\n   395→\n   396→            path (str): Scopes the cookie to the given path plus any\n   397→                subdirectories under that path (the \"/\" character is\n   398→                interpreted as a directory separator). If the cookie\n   399→                does not specify a path, the user agent defaults to the\n   400→                path component of the requested URI.\n   401→\n   402→                Warning:\n   403→                    User agent interfaces do not always isolate\n   404→                    cookies by path, and so this should not be\n   405→                    considered an effective security measure.\n   406→\n   407→                (See also: RFC 6265, Section 4.1.2.4)\n   408→\n   409→            secure (bool): Direct the client to only return the cookie\n   410→                in subsequent requests if they are made over HTTPS\n   411→                (default: ``True``). This prevents attackers from\n   412→                reading sensitive cookie data.\n   413→\n   414→                Note:\n   415→                    The default value for this argument is normally\n   416→                    ``True``, but can be modified by setting\n   417→                    :py:attr:`~.ResponseOptions.secure_cookies_by_default`\n   418→                    via :any:`App.resp_options`.\n   419→\n   420→                Warning:\n   421→                    For the `secure` cookie attribute to be effective,\n   422→                    your application will need to enforce HTTPS.\n   423→\n   424→                (See also: RFC 6265, Section 4.1.2.5)\n   425→\n   426→            http_only (bool): The HttpOnly attribute limits the scope of the\n   427→                cookie to HTTP requests.  In particular, the attribute\n   428→                instructs the user agent to omit the cookie when providing\n   429→                access to cookies via \"non-HTTP\" APIs. This is intended to\n   430→                mitigate some forms of cross-site scripting. (default: ``True``)\n   431→\n   432→                Note:\n   433→                    HttpOnly cookies are not visible to javascript scripts\n   434→                    in the browser. They are automatically sent to the server\n   435→                    on javascript ``XMLHttpRequest`` or ``Fetch`` requests.\n   436→\n   437→                (See also: RFC 6265, Section 4.1.2.6)\n   438→\n   439→            same_site (str): Helps protect against CSRF attacks by restricting\n   440→                when a cookie will be attached to the request by the user agent.\n   441→                When set to ``'Strict'``, the cookie will only be sent along\n   442→                with \"same-site\" requests.  If the value is ``'Lax'``, the\n   443→                cookie will be sent with same-site requests, and with\n   444→                \"cross-site\" top-level navigations.  If the value is ``'None'``,\n   445→                the cookie will be sent with same-site and cross-site requests.\n   446→                Finally, when this attribute is not set on the cookie, the\n   447→                attribute will be treated as if it had been set to ``'None'``.\n   448→\n   449→                (See also: `Same-Site RFC Draft`_)\n   450→\n   451→            partitioned (bool): Sets the Partitioned attribute indicating that\n   452→                the cookie is partitioned (CHIPS). When ``True``, the \"Partitioned\"\n   453→                flag will be added to the Set-Cookie header. If not provided,\n   454→                and ``same_site`` is ``'None'`` and the cookie is secure, Falcon\n   455→                will automatically set Partitioned to ensure compatibility with\n   456→                browsers that block third-party cookies.\n   457→\n   458→        Raises:\n   459→            KeyError: `name` is not a valid cookie name.\n   460→            ValueError: `value` is not a valid cookie value.\n   461→\n   462→        .. _RFC 6265:\n   463→            http://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc6265\n   464→\n   465→        .. _Same-Site RFC Draft:\n   466→            https://tools.ietf.org/html/draft-ietf-httpbis-rfc6265bis-03#section-4.1.2.7\n   467→\n   468→        \"\"\"\n   469→\n   470→        if not is_ascii_encodable(name):\n   471→            raise KeyError('name is not ascii encodable')\n   472→        if not is_ascii_encodable(value):\n   473→            raise ValueError('value is not ascii encodable')\n   474→\n   475→        value = str(value)\n   476→\n   477→        if self._cookies is None:\n   478→            self._cookies = http_cookies.SimpleCookie()\n   479→\n   480→        try:\n   481→            self._cookies[name] = value\n   482→        except http_cookies.CookieError as e:  # pragma: no cover\n   483→            # NOTE(tbug): we raise a KeyError here, to avoid leaking\n   484→            # the CookieError to the user. SimpleCookie (well, BaseCookie)\n   485→            # only throws CookieError on issues with the cookie key\n   486→            raise KeyError(str(e))\n   487→\n   488→        if expires:\n   489→            # set Expires on cookie. Format is Wdy, DD Mon YYYY HH:MM:SS GMT\n   490→\n   491→            # NOTE(tbug): we never actually need to\n   492→            # know that GMT is named GMT when formatting cookies.\n   493→            # It is a function call less to just write \"GMT\" in the fmt string:\n   494→            fmt = '%a, %d %b %Y %H:%M:%S GMT'\n   495→            if expires.tzinfo is None:\n   496→                # naive\n   497→                self._cookies[name]['expires'] = expires.strftime(fmt)\n   498→            else:\n   499→                # aware\n   500→                gmt_expires = expires.astimezone(GMT_TIMEZONE)\n   501→                self._cookies[name]['expires'] = gmt_expires.strftime(fmt)\n   502→\n   503→        if max_age:\n   504→            # RFC 6265 section 5.2.2 says about the max-age value:\n   505→            #   \"If the remainder of attribute-value contains a non-DIGIT\n   506→            #    character, ignore the cookie-av.\"\n   507→            # That is, RFC-compliant response parsers will ignore the max-age\n   508→            # attribute if the value contains a dot, as in floating point\n   509→            # numbers. Therefore, attempt to convert the value to an integer.\n   510→            self._cookies[name]['max-age'] = int(max_age)\n   511→\n   512→        if domain:\n   513→            self._cookies[name]['domain'] = domain\n   514→\n   515→        if path:\n   516→            self._cookies[name]['path'] = path\n   517→\n   518→        is_secure = self.options.secure_cookies_by_default if secure is None else secure\n   519→\n   520→        if is_secure:\n   521→            self._cookies[name]['secure'] = True\n   522→\n   523→        if http_only:\n   524→            self._cookies[name]['httponly'] = http_only\n   525→\n   526→        # PERF(kgriffs): Morsel.__setitem__() will lowercase this anyway,\n   527→        #   so we can just pass this in and when __setitem__() calls\n   528→        #   lower() it will be very slightly faster.\n   529→        auto_partitioned = False\n   530→        if same_site:\n   531→            same_site = same_site.lower()\n   532→\n   533→            if same_site not in _RESERVED_SAMESITE_VALUES:\n   534→                raise ValueError(\n   535→                    \"same_site must be set to either 'lax', 'strict', or 'none'\"\n   536→                )\n   537→\n   538→            self._cookies[name]['samesite'] = same_site.capitalize()\n   539→\n   540→            # Per CHIPS, when SameSite=None and Secure, auto-set Partitioned\n   541→            if same_site == 'none' and is_secure:\n   542→                auto_partitioned = True\n   543→\n   544→        # Explicit partitioned flag support, or auto when applicable\n   545→        if partitioned or auto_partitioned:\n   546→            # Morsel treats flag attributes by setting the key to True\n   547→            # The actual header will render 'Partitioned' without value\n   548→            self._cookies[name]['partitioned'] = True\n   549→\n   550→    def unset_cookie(self, name, samesite='Lax', domain=None, path=None):\n   551→        \"\"\"Unset a cookie in the response.\n   552→\n   553→        Clears the contents of the cookie, and instructs the user\n   554→        agent to immediately expire its own copy of the cookie.\n   555→\n   556→        Note:\n   557→            Modern browsers place restriction on cookies without the\n   558→            \"same-site\" cookie attribute set. To that end this attribute\n   559→            is set to ``'Lax'`` by this method.\n   560→\n   561→            (See also: `Same-Site warnings`_)\n   562→\n   563→        Warning:\n   564→            In order to successfully remove a cookie, both the\n   565→            path and the domain must match the values that were\n   566→            used when the cookie was created.\n   567→\n   568→        Args:\n   569→            name (str): Cookie name\n   570→\n   571→        Keyword Args:\n   572→            samesite (str): Allows to override the default 'Lax' same_site\n   573→                    setting for the unset cookie.\n   574→\n   575→            domain (str): Restricts the cookie to a specific domain and\n   576→                    any subdomains of that domain. By default, the user\n   577→                    agent will return the cookie only to the origin server.\n   578→                    When overriding this default behavior, the specified\n   579→                    domain must include the origin server. Otherwise, the\n   580→                    user agent will reject the cookie.\n   581→\n   582→                    Note:\n   583→                        Cookies do not provide isolation by port, so the domain\n   584→                        should not provide one. (See also: RFC 6265, Section 8.5)\n   585→\n   586→                    (See also: RFC 6265, Section 4.1.2.3)\n   587→\n   588→            path (str): Scopes the cookie to the given path plus any\n   589→                subdirectories under that path (the \"/\" character is\n   590→                interpreted as a directory separator). If the cookie\n   591→                does not specify a path, the user agent defaults to the\n   592→                path component of the requested URI.\n   593→\n   594→                Warning:\n   595→                    User agent interfaces do not always isolate\n   596→                    cookies by path, and so this should not be\n   597→                    considered an effective security measure.\n   598→\n   599→                (See also: RFC 6265, Section 4.1.2.4)\n   600→\n   601→        .. _Same-Site warnings:\n   602→            https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Headers/Set-Cookie/SameSite#Fixing_common_warnings\n   603→        \"\"\"  # noqa: E501\n   604→        if self._cookies is None:\n   605→            self._cookies = http_cookies.SimpleCookie()\n   606→\n   607→        self._cookies[name] = ''\n   608→\n   609→        # NOTE(Freezerburn): SimpleCookie apparently special cases the\n   610→        # expires attribute to automatically use strftime and set the\n   611→        # time as a delta from the current time. We use -1 here to\n   612→        # basically tell the browser to immediately expire the cookie,\n   613→        # thus removing it from future request objects.\n   614→        self._cookies[name]['expires'] = -1\n   615→\n   616→        # NOTE(CaselIT): Set SameSite to Lax to avoid setting invalid cookies.\n   617→        # See https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Headers/Set-Cookie/SameSite#Fixing_common_warnings  # noqa: E501\n   618→        self._cookies[name]['samesite'] = samesite\n   619→\n   620→        if domain:\n   621→            self._cookies[name]['domain'] = domain\n   622→\n   623→        if path:\n   624→            self._cookies[name]['path'] = path\n   625→\n   626→    def get_header(self, name, default=None):\n   627→        \"\"\"Retrieve the raw string value for the given header.\n   628→\n   629→        Normally, when a header has multiple values, they will be\n   630→        returned as a single, comma-delimited string. However, the\n   631→        Set-Cookie header does not support this format, and so\n   632→        attempting to retrieve it will raise an error.\n   633→\n   634→        Args:\n   635→            name (str): Header name, case-insensitive. Must be of type ``str``\n   636→                or ``StringType``, and only character values 0x00 through 0xFF\n   637→                may be used on platforms that use wide characters.\n   638→        Keyword Args:\n   639→            default: Value to return if the header\n   640→                is not found (default ``None``).\n   641→\n   642→        Raises:\n   643→            ValueError: The value of the 'Set-Cookie' header(s) was requested.\n   644→\n   645→        Returns:\n   646→            str: The value of the specified header if set, or\n   647→            the default value if not set.\n   648→        \"\"\"\n   649→\n   650→        # NOTE(kgriffs): normalize name by lowercasing it\n   651→        name = name.lower()\n   652→\n   653→        if name == 'set-cookie':\n   654→            raise HeaderNotSupported('Getting Set-Cookie is not currently supported.')\n   655→\n   656→        return self._headers.get(name, default)\n   657→\n   658→    def set_header(self, name, value):\n   659→        \"\"\"Set a header for this response to a given value.\n   660→\n   661→        Warning:\n   662→            Calling this method overwrites any values already set for this\n   663→            header. To append an additional value for this header, use\n   664→            :meth:`~.append_header` instead.\n   665→\n   666→        Warning:\n   667→            This method cannot be used to set cookies; instead, use\n   668→            :meth:`~.append_header` or :meth:`~.set_cookie`.\n   669→\n   670→        Args:\n   671→            name (str): Header name (case-insensitive). The name may contain\n   672→                only US-ASCII characters.\n   673→            value (str): Value for the header. As with the header's name, the\n   674→                value may contain only US-ASCII characters.\n   675→\n   676→        Raises:\n   677→            ValueError: `name` cannot be ``'Set-Cookie'``.\n   678→        \"\"\"\n   679→\n   680→        # NOTE(kgriffs): uwsgi fails with a TypeError if any header\n   681→        # is not a str, so do the conversion here. It's actually\n   682→        # faster to not do an isinstance check. str() will encode\n   683→        # to US-ASCII.\n   684→        value = str(value)\n   685→\n   686→        # NOTE(kgriffs): normalize name by lowercasing it\n   687→        name = name.lower()\n   688→\n   689→        if name == 'set-cookie':\n   690→            raise HeaderNotSupported('This method cannot be used to set cookies')\n   691→\n   692→        self._headers[name] = value\n   693→\n   694→    def delete_header(self, name):\n   695→        \"\"\"Delete a header that was previously set for this response.\n   696→\n   697→        If the header was not previously set, nothing is done (no error is\n   698→        raised). Otherwise, all values set for the header will be removed\n   699→        from the response.\n   700→\n   701→        Note that calling this method is equivalent to setting the\n   702→        corresponding header property (when said property is available) to\n   703→        ``None``. For example::\n   704→\n   705→            resp.etag = None\n   706→\n   707→        Warning:\n   708→            This method cannot be used with the Set-Cookie header. Instead,\n   709→            use :meth:`~.unset_cookie` to remove a cookie and ensure that the\n   710→            user agent expires its own copy of the data as well.\n   711→\n   712→        Args:\n   713→            name (str): Header name (case-insensitive). The name may\n   714→                contain only US-ASCII characters.\n   715→\n   716→        Raises:\n   717→            ValueError: `name` cannot be ``'Set-Cookie'``.\n   718→        \"\"\"\n   719→\n   720→        # NOTE(kgriffs): normalize name by lowercasing it\n   721→        name = name.lower()\n   722→\n   723→        if name == 'set-cookie':\n   724→            raise HeaderNotSupported('This method cannot be used to remove cookies')\n   725→\n   726→        self._headers.pop(name, None)\n   727→\n   728→    def append_header(self, name, value):\n   729→        \"\"\"Set or append a header for this response.\n   730→\n   731→        If the header already exists, the new value will normally be appended\n   732→        to it, delimited by a comma. The notable exception to this rule is\n   733→        Set-Cookie, in which case a separate header line for each value will be\n   734→        included in the response.\n   735→\n   736→        Note:\n   737→            While this method can be used to efficiently append raw\n   738→            Set-