 the stream is known in advance. Using this\n   221\u2192        method is also slightly more performant as compared to setting the\n   222\u2192        properties individually.\n   223\u2192\n   224\u2192        Note:\n   225\u2192            If the stream length is unknown, you can set\n   226\u2192            :attr:`~falcon.asgi.Response.stream` directly, and ignore\n   227\u2192            :attr:`~falcon.asgi.Response.content_length`. In this case, the ASGI\n   228\u2192            server may choose to use chunked encoding for HTTP/1.1\n   229\u2192\n   230\u2192        Args:\n   231\u2192            stream: A readable, awaitable file-like object or async iterable\n   232\u2192                that returns byte strings. If the object implements a close()\n   233\u2192                method, it will be called after reading all of the data.\n   234\u2192            content_length (int): Length of the stream, used for the\n   235\u2192                Content-Length header in the response.\n   236\u2192        \"\"\"\n   237\u2192\n   238\u2192        self.stream = stream\n   239\u2192\n   240\u2192        # PERF(kgriffs): Set directly rather than incur the overhead of\n   241\u2192        #   the self.content_length property.\n   242\u2192        self._headers['content-length'] = str(content_length)\n   243\u2192\n   244\u2192    async def render_body(self):\n   245\u2192        \"\"\"Get the raw bytestring content for the response body.\n   246\u2192\n   247\u2192        This coroutine can be awaited to get the raw data for the\n   248\u2192        HTTP response body, taking into account the :attr:`~.text`,\n   249\u2192        :attr:`~.data`, and :attr:`~.media` attributes.\n   250\u2192\n   251\u2192        Note:\n   252\u2192            This method ignores :attr:`~.stream`; the caller must check\n   253\u2192            and handle that attribute directly.\n   254\u2192\n   255\u2192        Returns:\n   256\u2192            bytes: The UTF-8 encoded value of the `text` attribute, if\n   257\u2192            set. Otherwise, the value of the `data` attribute if set, or\n   258\u2192            finally the serialized value of the `media` attribute. If\n   259\u2192            none of these attributes are set, ``None`` is returned.\n   260\u2192        \"\"\"\n   261\u2192\n   262\u2192        # NOTE(vytas): The code below is also inlined in asgi.App.__call__.\n   263\u2192\n   264\u2192        text = self.text\n   265\u2192        if text is None:\n   266\u2192            data = self._data\n   267\u2192\n   268\u2192            if data is None and self._media is not None:\n   269\u2192                # NOTE(kgriffs): We use a special _UNSET singleton since\n   270\u2192                #   None is ambiguous (the media handler might return None).\n   271\u2192                if self._media_rendered is _UNSET:\n   272\u2192                    if not self.content_type:\n   273\u2192                        self.content_type = self.options.default_media_type\n   274\u2192\n   275\u2192                    handler, serialize_sync, _ = self.options.media_handlers._resolve(\n   276\u2192                        self.content_type, self.options.default_media_type\n   277\u2192                    )\n   278\u2192\n   279\u2192                    if serialize_sync:\n   280\u2192                        self._media_rendered = serialize_sync(self._media)\n   281\u2192                    else:\n   282\u2192                        self._media_rendered = await handler.serialize_async(\n   283\u2192                            self._media, self.content_type\n   284\u2192                        )\n   285\u2192\n   286\u2192                data = self._media_rendered\n   287\u2192        else:\n   288\u2192            try:\n   289\u2192                # NOTE(kgriffs): Normally we expect text to be a string\n   290\u2192                data = text.encode()\n   291\u2192            except AttributeError:\n   292\u2192                # NOTE(kgriffs): Assum